      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So if the precision is limited, it's basically saying the data is just \"noisy\" and unpredictable. It feels almost like there's a gap between the math and what's actually happening in the real world. Is there a technical way to describe that lack of stability, or do I just have to admit that there's a lot of random fluctuation interfering with the results?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A technical way to describe that lack of stability, beyond just saying it's \"noisy,\" is to discuss the **width of the confidence interval** relative to the **effect size itself**. A wide CI, even with a low p-value, signals low statistical power or high variance in your data. You are essentially saying that your estimate is imprecise.\n\nInstead of just admitting random fluctuation, you can argue that the uncertainty surrounding the effect is high. For instance, you could state that \"the substantial width of the 95% CI suggests considerable sampling variability, indicating that while a statistically significant difference was detected, the magnitude of that difference remains highly uncertain.\" This sounds much more rigorous than just blaming random fluctuation.",
      "source": "target",
